WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.700
How does the tip of a growing axon know which way to turn?

2
00:00:07.683 --> 00:00:12.323
An axon is the long fibre a nerve cell sends out to reach its partner.

3
00:00:13.333 --> 00:00:16.533
At its end is a flat, moving fan called the growth cone.

4
00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:20.027
Microtubules fill the centre of the fan.

5
00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:23.727
Actin fills its edge, in thin spikes and flat sheets.

6
00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:26.437
The spikes are filopodia.

7
00:00:27.197 --> 00:00:30.677
They are feelers and feet at once, sampling the ground and gripping it.

8
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:36.600
Molecules outside the cell, called guidance cues, attract or repel the growth cone.

9
00:00:37.633 --> 00:00:42.293
In a classic test, a fine pipette releases a cue beside a living growth cone.

10
00:00:43.317 --> 00:00:44.837
The cue thins with distance.

11
00:00:45.617 --> 00:00:48.257
So the near side meets a little more than the far side.

12
00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:53.967
How much more? In the standard assay, only about five to ten percent.

13
00:00:54.967 --> 00:00:57.527
Now flood the whole dish with ten times more cue.

14
00:00:58.347 --> 00:00:59.727
Do growth cones turn harder?

15
00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:09.270
No. Across many cones there is no net turn.

16
00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.690
There is more cue, but no difference between the sides.

17
00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.570
So the difference across the width gives the direction.

18
00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:23.250
The overall amount changes how strongly cones respond.

19
00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:27.910
Here is the rule, for a growth cone on a flat surface in a dish.

20
00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:33.330
The edge moves forward by the actin added there, minus the actin that slides back.

21
00:01:34.333 --> 00:01:37.533
Grip on the ground, the clutch, holds back part of that slide.

22
00:01:38.567 --> 00:01:42.367
A turn sticks where microtubules move in and build new axon.

23
00:01:43.247 --> 00:01:45.447
Microtubules can also start the turn themselves.

24
00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:47.947
Three questions follow.

25
00:01:47.947 --> 00:01:52.247
How can a tip move forward while much of its actin streams backward?

26
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.520
How does so small a difference become a turn of the whole axon?

27
00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.810
And can the tip make that decision on its own?

28
00:02:00.733 --> 00:02:06.453
Carry one idea. A cue gives a direction by making the two sides of the growth cone

29
00:02:06.453 --> 00:02:06.693
differ.
